WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.821
Last time we said that Big-O

2
00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.750
drops constant factors.

3
00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.540
Is that always honest?

4
00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:10.580
Take two programs.

5
00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:14.200
Program P takes one hundred n steps.

6
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.650
Program Q takes n squared steps.

7
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.366
With ten items, P needs a thousand steps

8
00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.830
and Q only a hundred.

9
00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:25.070
Q wins.

10
00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:28.170
At a hundred items they tie

11
00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:30.250
ten thousand steps each.

12
00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:32.726
At a thousand items

13
00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:34.867
P needs a hundred thousand steps

14
00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:37.630
and Q needs a million.

15
00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:39.120
P wins.

16
00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:43.990
P is big-O of n, Q is big-O of n squared

17
00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.290
and Big-O says P is better.

18
00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:49.390
Yet for small inputs, Q was faster.

19
00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.480
So who is right?

20
00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:52.980
Both.

21
00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.000
To see why

22
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.020
we have to say exactly what Big-O claims.

23
00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:59.310
And then we can prove it.

24
00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:01.433
In this video we'll see

25
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:02.980
the formal definition

26
00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:04.475
and what a witness is

27
00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.498
how to prove a bound and

28
00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:09.420
how to disprove one, and the full family

29
00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.930
big-O, omega and theta.

30
00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:14.206
Here is the definition.

31
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:18.623
We write f of n is big-O of g of n if

32
00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:21.680
there are constants c and n zero

33
00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.440
such that f of n is at most c times g of n

34
00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.870
for every n from n zero onward.

35
00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.550
Two constants, and a promise

36
00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:32.490
from n zero on

37
00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:35.310
f never rises above c times g.

38
00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.870
We call the pair c and n zero a witness.

39
00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:41.670
To prove f is big-O of g

40
00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:42.690
you show a witness.

41
00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:44.500
That is the whole proof.

42
00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:46.266
Notice what the definition

43
00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.420
says about small n.

44
00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:49.482
Nothing.

45
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:51.162
Everything before n zero is

46
00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:52.400
allowed to break the rule.

47
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.600
Keep that in mind

48
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.010
it is half of the answer to our question.

49
00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:58.917
Let's prove that three n squared plus

50
00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.280
five n is big-O of n squared.

51
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.310
We need to find c and n zero.

52
00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.350
The five n term is the problem.

53
00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:11.190
But for any n that is at least five

54
00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.590
five n is at most n squared

55
00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.530
because n squared is n times n

56
00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:18.740
and n is at least five.

57
00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.860
So for n at least five

58
00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.513
three n squared plus five n is at most

59
00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.500
three n squared plus n squared

60
00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.130
which is four n squared.

61
00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.030
There is our witness: c equals four

62
00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:35.050
and n zero equals five.

63
00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:36.901
Check it at n equals five.

64
00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:39.338
Three times twenty-five plus five

65
00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:41.834
times five is exactly a hundred

66
00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:43.354
and four times twenty-five

67
00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.010
is also a hundred.

68
00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:46.530
Right on the edge.

69
00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.090
At n equals four it breaks

70
00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:51.930
sixty-eight is more than sixty-four.

71
00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:54.490
So with c equal to four

72
00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:57.640
five is the smallest n zero that works.

73
00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.690
And with c equal to three?

74
00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:03.930
It can never work, because of the five n.

75
00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:05.390
Witnesses are not unique

76
00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:07.680
but c has to be large enough.

77
00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:11.430
How do you show that something is not big-O?

78
00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.670
You assume a witness exists

79
00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:15.538
and find a contradiction.

80
00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:17.613
Let's show that n cubed is

81
00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:20.020
not big-O of n squared.

82
00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:23.220
Suppose some c and n zero work.

83
00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:26.880
Then n cubed is at most c times n squared

84
00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.570
for all n from n zero on.

85
00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:32.688
Now pick n to be the larger

86
00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:35.610
of n zero and c plus one.

87
00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:39.230
Then n cubed is n times n squared

88
00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:41.230
and n is bigger than c.

89
00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:45.890
So n cubed is bigger than c times n squared.

90
00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:47.880
That contradicts the witness.

91
00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:51.490
No constant can rescue n cubed.

92
00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:53.509
However large c is

93
00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:56.138
n cubed overtakes c times n squared

94
00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:58.280
as soon as n passes c.

95
00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.810
Big-O is an upper bound.

96
00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:02.700
It has two companions.

97
00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:05.500
Omega is the lower bound

98
00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:09.060
f is at least c times g from some point on.

99
00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:11.180
And theta is both at once

100
00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:14.620
f is squeezed between two multiples of g.

101
00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:17.250
Theta says g is the exact shape.

102
00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.172
For our example

103
00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:20.973
three n squared is below three

104
00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:22.430
n squared plus five n

105
00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:24.870
which is below four n squared

106
00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:26.450
from n equals five on.

107
00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:29.430
So the function is theta of n squared

108
00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:30.440
not just big-O.

109
00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:32.244
One habit to watch.

110
00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:34.910
People say this algorithm is big-O of

111
00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.576
n squared when they mean theta.

112
00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.690
But big-O alone only promises

113
00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:41.091
an upper limit.

114
00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.079
An algorithm that takes n steps

115
00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:45.400
is also big-O of n squared.

116
00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:47.510
True, but not useful.

117
00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:49.782
Here are the shapes that come

118
00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.160
up again and again

119
00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.090
from slowest growing to fastest growing.

120
00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:55.490
One.

121
00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:56.950
Log n.

122
00:04:56.950 --> 00:04:57.950
n.

123
00:04:57.950 --> 00:04:59.390
n log n.

124
00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:00.990
n squared.

125
00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:02.400
And two to the n.

126
00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:04.900
To see how far apart they are

127
00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:07.660
take n equal to twenty.

128
00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:08.360
One.

129
00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:10.400
About four.

130
00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:11.100
Twenty.

131
00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.580
About eighty-six.

132
00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:14.800
Four hundred.

133
00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:18.150
And two to the twenty is over a million.

134
00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:20.465
Each class beats the one before

135
00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:22.850
it once n is large enough.

136
00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:25.024
That is why we can compare algorithms

137
00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:26.310
by their shape.

138
00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:28.500
Now back to the question.

139
00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:31.170
Is it honest to drop constants?

140
00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:34.870
As a statement about large n, yes.

141
00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:39.030
Program P, a hundred n, beats Q, n squared

142
00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:40.760
once n passes a hundred.

143
00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:43.820
But if P had been a million n

144
00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:47.180
the crossover would be at a million items.

145
00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.020
For smaller inputs

146
00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:51.470
the n squared program would be faster.

147
00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:55.127
So dropping constants becomes a lie only

148
00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:57.470
if you forget what Big-O promises

149
00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:00.810
behavior from n zero onward.

150
00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:03.450
Where n zero is can be huge

151
00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:05.450
and the definition does not tell you.

152
00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:07.213
When inputs are small

153
00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:08.754
or the constants differ by

154
00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:11.580
orders of magnitude, measure.

155
00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:13.510
To sum up.

156
00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:15.990
Big-O has a precise definition

157
00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:18.150
constants c and n zero

158
00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:22.080
and f below c times g from n zero on.

159
00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:24.960
To prove a bound, find a witness.

160
00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:26.380
To disprove one

161
00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:28.950
assume a witness and find a contradiction.

162
00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:32.670
Omega is the lower bound, theta is both

163
00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:35.540
and Big-O promises nothing about small n.

164
00:06:35.890 --> 00:06:38.420
Next, we'll look at another kind of cost

165
00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:41.020
what one operation costs on average

166
00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:43.640
when most are cheap and a few are expensive.

167
00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:46.050
That is amortized analysis.

168
00:06:46.677 --> 00:06:49.387
Now let's turn the definition into code

169
00:06:49.387 --> 00:06:51.757
in Python, and test our own proofs.

170
00:06:52.337 --> 00:06:54.085
The function first_violation

171
00:06:54.147 --> 00:06:56.467
takes two functions, f and g

172
00:06:56.467 --> 00:06:59.747
the constants c and n zero, and a limit.

173
00:06:59.747 --> 00:07:03.307
It walks n from n zero up to the limit.

174
00:07:03.307 --> 00:07:06.567
If f of n is above c times g of n

175
00:07:06.567 --> 00:07:09.687
that n breaks the witness, so we return it.

176
00:07:09.687 --> 00:07:12.177
If nothing breaks, it returns None.

177
00:07:12.527 --> 00:07:14.526
is_witness just asks whether

178
00:07:14.597 --> 00:07:16.157
there is no violation.

179
00:07:16.157 --> 00:07:20.007
And ratio gives f of n divided by g of n.

180
00:07:20.587 --> 00:07:22.070
Now our examples.

181
00:07:22.157 --> 00:07:24.269
With c equal to four and

182
00:07:24.357 --> 00:07:27.917
n zero equal to five, is_witness says True

183
00:07:27.917 --> 00:07:30.307
all the way up to ten thousand.

184
00:07:30.657 --> 00:07:32.907
With n zero equal to four

185
00:07:32.907 --> 00:07:35.887
the first violation is n equal to four

186
00:07:35.887 --> 00:07:37.807
exactly as we found by hand.

187
00:07:37.807 --> 00:07:39.687
With c equal to three

188
00:07:39.687 --> 00:07:42.627
the first violation is n equal to one.

189
00:07:42.627 --> 00:07:44.337
It never works.

190
00:07:44.687 --> 00:07:47.982
And for n cubed against a hundred n squared

191
00:07:48.057 --> 00:07:50.205
the first violation is n equal

192
00:07:50.277 --> 00:07:51.817
to one hundred and one.

193
00:07:51.817 --> 00:07:54.727
Just after c, as the proof predicted.

194
00:07:55.307 --> 00:07:57.291
This table shows f of n

195
00:07:57.377 --> 00:07:59.037
divided by n squared.

196
00:07:59.037 --> 00:08:01.357
It starts at three and a half

197
00:08:01.357 --> 00:08:02.977
and settles toward three

198
00:08:02.977 --> 00:08:05.697
three point oh five, three point oh oh five

199
00:08:05.697 --> 00:08:07.626
three point oh oh oh five.

200
00:08:07.697 --> 00:08:09.731
That is what theta of n squared

201
00:08:09.797 --> 00:08:11.477
looks like in numbers.

202
00:08:11.477 --> 00:08:14.267
The ratio stays between two constants.

203
00:08:14.617 --> 00:08:18.487
The last column is n cubed over n squared.

204
00:08:18.487 --> 00:08:21.907
It is just n, and it keeps growing.

205
00:08:21.907 --> 00:08:23.457
No constant bounds it.

206
00:08:24.037 --> 00:08:25.207
One warning.

207
00:08:25.207 --> 00:08:27.947
This code checks only up to the limit.

208
00:08:27.947 --> 00:08:30.907
If is_witness returns True

209
00:08:30.907 --> 00:08:34.037
that is evidence, not a proof.

210
00:08:34.387 --> 00:08:36.917
With c equal to a hundred

211
00:08:36.917 --> 00:08:39.537
n cubed passes the check up to a hundred.

212
00:08:39.537 --> 00:08:41.942
And fails at a hundred and one.

213
00:08:42.017 --> 00:08:43.997
The proof is the algebra from the

214
00:08:44.057 --> 00:08:45.257
first half of this video.

215
00:08:45.257 --> 00:08:47.537
The code helps you find a witness

216
00:08:47.537 --> 00:08:50.427
or find the n where your guess breaks.

217
00:08:51.007 --> 00:08:53.377
So you now have the definition

218
00:08:53.377 --> 00:08:54.937
a proof by witness

219
00:08:54.937 --> 00:08:56.131
a proof by contradiction

220
00:08:56.177 --> 00:08:58.177
and a small tool to check

221
00:08:58.257 --> 00:08:59.687
your guesses in Python.

222
00:09:00.037 --> 00:09:03.037
Next time, amortized analysis.

